# Personal website and Sentinel Grid frontend

This is a static GitHub Pages site. `index.html` contains the portfolio and AI/cyber news center; `sentinel.html` contains the dedicated MapLibre intelligence map.

## Frontend structure

- `assets/js/site.js` — portfolio, project viewer, background rotation, and AI/cyber feed
- `assets/js/sentinel-data.js` — Sentinel manifest/health publication client shared by both pages
- `assets/js/sentinel-home.js` — Sentinel status summary on the homepage
- `assets/js/sentinel-map.js` — dedicated map rendering, filters, layers, and event details
- `assets/css/sentinel-map.css` — map-only styling
- `data/` — static data synchronized by `.github/workflows/sync-states.yml`

No build step or paid service is required. Serve the repository with any static HTTP server for local testing; opening files directly can prevent browsers from fetching local JSON.

## Data workflow architecture

Each monitor publishes its own independent output. The website never generates or
combines monitor intelligence:

AI Cyber Daily Digest repository
-> `AI & Cyber Daily Digest` generates and validates `data/ai_cyber_digest.json`
-> commits only changed, non-empty output

Other monitor repositories
-> generate their own JSON files
-> commit valid output independently

Website `Sync Monitor States`
-> runs three times daily and pulls the newest valid source files available
-> checks out disaster and Sentinel sources independently
-> validates every available file before atomic replacement
-> preserves last-known-good website files after unavailable or invalid input
-> commits only changed public website data

Website `Sync AI & Cyber Daily Digest`
-> runs after the standalone AI digest workflow
-> copies only the completed public `data/ai_cyber_digest.json`
-> validates non-empty output and preserves the last-known-good website copy

`Deploy Website`
-> publishes the website after repository updates

The website continues fetching AI, Sentinel, disaster, environmental, and EWS
JSON independently. Internal AI digest state and Markdown output are not copied
by the website sync.

## Schedule

GitHub cron expressions use UTC. The daily digest and monitor-state workflows
include both possible EST and EDT hours, then use an `America/New_York` guard to
prevent a duplicate run.

- AI & Cyber Daily Digest: approximately 9:00 AM Eastern daily.
- Sync AI & Cyber Daily Digest: approximately 9:10 AM Eastern daily.
- Sync Monitor States: approximately 9:10 AM, 3:10 PM, and 9:10 PM Eastern.
- Sync Early Warning System: every 10 minutes. Manual and repository-dispatch
  triggers are supported, but the current EWS source does not emit that dispatch.
- Sentinel Grid source collection: its existing `17 */6 * * *` UTC schedule;
  it has not been changed to the requested 9:00 AM, 3:00 PM, and 9:00 PM
  Eastern source windows.
- Disaster monitor source collection: its existing frequent schedule. It has
  not been changed or coordinated by this website repository.
- Deploy Website: every push to `main`.

All sync workflows retain `workflow_dispatch` for manual runs. GitHub may delay
a scheduled job; the sync always uses the newest valid source publication
available when it starts.
The source-generation schedules are not yet fully aligned with the three daily
website synchronization slots.

## Retention and last-known-good behavior

Time-sensitive retention belongs in each source repository, before publication.
The website sync does not rewrite intelligence records or apply a blanket cutoff.
Seven-day public event retention has not yet been implemented. It must be added
later in Sentinel Grid, the disaster monitor, and any other source repository
that produces recent-event data. Sentinel trends and internal history must
remain longer than seven days, and this website must not delete source
intelligence records. The standalone AI digest already applies its own story
count and age limits.

`scripts/sync-monitor-data.py` validates downloaded JSON and expected structures.
Sentinel files are additionally checked against the publication manifest's byte
counts and SHA-256 hashes. A missing, malformed, checksum-mismatched, or
unexpectedly empty source is skipped without replacing the website's current
copy. The workflow summary reports updated, unchanged, skipped, and failed files
along with output sizes and freshness warnings.

## Troubleshooting

1. Run the relevant source workflow manually and confirm it committed valid
   output.
2. Run `Sync Monitor States` or `Sync Early Warning System` with
   `workflow_dispatch`.
3. Inspect the job summary for per-file validation failures or stale-source
   warnings.
4. Confirm `REPO_ACCESS_TOKEN` can read each private source repository.
5. Run `python -m unittest discover -s tests -v` locally before changing sync
   validation.
## X report contract

The shared X ingestion contract is documented in
[`docs/X_SOURCES_SCHEMA.md`](docs/X_SOURCES_SCHEMA.md). The website validates
the complete versioned document before replacing `data/x_sources.json`;
invalid, expired, duplicate, or schema-incompatible reports are rejected
without replacing the last known good feed.
## X source publication workflow

The X map-data workflow is event-driven and does not independently poll the
producer. A successful `x-sources` publication sends an
`x-sources-published` event containing its immutable commit SHA, slot and
generation identifiers, schema version, report count, and completion time.

The website checks out that exact source commit, cross-checks the event against
the source manifest and feed, regenerates the X event/GeoJSON pair, and records
the completed generation in `data/x_source_sync_state.json`. Exact replays are
harmless. A conflicting generation for a completed slot fails unless a manual
recovery run explicitly sets `force`.

Configure the website secret `REPO_ACCESS_TOKEN` with read access to the private
`SithOperation/x-sources` repository. Manual recovery requires the same
immutable metadata found in the producer manifest and commit history; it never
follows the moving `main` branch.
